     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 7,650 mg/day of metformin is three times the maximum labeled dose, and no indication supports it. Doses that high carry a serious risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function. Higher doses also don't improve glycemic control, because the effect plateaus around 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** That is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum, and may be what you intended.\n- **Enter a different dose or frequency within the recommended range.** For example, 1,000 mg PO twice daily, or a lower dose to titrate up.\n- **Add a note to the order** if you want to document your intent.\n\nIf you have a clinical reason for going above the maximum, please discuss it with pharmacy and have the order go through your institution's override and verification process. Tell me which of the options above you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
